1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:01,220
<u>Coroutines and Flow</u>

2
00:00:02,537 --> 00:00:05,227
<i>How do you use coroutines and Flow in an Android application?</i>

3
00:00:06,490 --> 00:00:11,580
I use coroutines for one-shot asynchronous work such as network requests, database operations, and parallel loading.

4
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:17,580
I use Flow for streams of updates such as UI state, Room observation, search, real-time data, and combining several sources.

5
00:00:19,094 --> 00:00:21,604
<i>What is a coroutine, and how is it different from a Thread?</i>

6
00:00:23,115 --> 00:00:26,735
A coroutine is a lightweight task that runs on top of threads and can suspend without blocking them.

7
00:00:27,445 --> 00:00:29,545
One thread can serve many coroutines over time.

8
00:00:31,081 --> 00:00:32,111
<i>What does suspend mean?</i>

9
00:00:33,643 --> 00:00:36,903
suspend means that a function may pause and continue later without blocking the thread.

10
00:00:37,603 --> 00:00:40,163
It does not automatically move the work to a background thread.

11
00:00:41,681 --> 00:00:43,531
<i>What is the difference between launch and async?</i>

12
00:00:45,054 --> 00:00:47,934
launch returns a Job and is used when no separate result is needed.

13
00:00:48,634 --> 00:00:51,664
async returns Deferred<T>, and its result is received with await().

14
00:00:53,169 --> 00:00:54,539
<i>What is structured concurrency?</i>

15
00:00:56,062 --> 00:00:59,232
Structured concurrency ties coroutines to a specific scope and lifecycle.

16
00:00:59,932 --> 00:01:02,462
A parent waits for its children and can cancel them as one group.

17
00:01:03,985 --> 00:01:06,795
<i>What is the difference between coroutineScope and supervisorScope?</i>

18
00:01:08,289 --> 00:01:11,719
In coroutineScope, a failure in one child usually cancels the other children.

19
00:01:12,429 --> 00:01:16,449
In supervisorScope, child tasks are isolated and can handle failures independently.

20
00:01:18,329 --> 00:01:19,539
<i>How does cancellation work?</i>

21
00:01:20,818 --> 00:01:25,408
Cancellation is cooperative: a coroutine reacts at suspension points or when it explicitly checks isActive.

22
00:01:26,108 --> 00:01:29,098
CancellationException normally should not be handled as a regular error.

23
00:01:30,609 --> 00:01:31,869
<i>What are the main Dispatchers?</i>

24
00:01:33,387 --> 00:01:37,827
Dispatchers.Main is used for UI work, IO for blocking I/O, and Default for CPU-intensive work.

25
00:01:38,527 --> 00:01:41,267
withContext() changes the context inside a suspend function.

26
00:01:42,798 --> 00:01:45,468
<i>Which synchronization tools are used with Kotlin Coroutines?</i>

27
00:01:46,987 --> 00:01:51,117
Common options are Mutex for protecting suspending code, atomic operations for simple shared state,

28
00:01:51,327 --> 00:01:53,427
and state confinement where one coroutine owns the state.

29
00:01:54,117 --> 00:01:57,647
synchronized is also possible, but suspend functions must not be called inside it.

30
00:01:59,177 --> 00:01:59,877
<i>What is Flow?</i>

31
00:02:01,403 --> 00:02:04,393
Flow is an asynchronous stream of values from the coroutines ecosystem.

32
00:02:05,103 --> 00:02:08,013
A regular Flow is cold: every collector starts the upstream again.

33
00:02:09,542 --> 00:02:11,312
<i>What is Channel in Kotlin Coroutines?</i>

34
00:02:12,843 --> 00:02:16,083
Channel is a hot coroutine primitive for sending values between coroutines.

35
00:02:16,783 --> 00:02:21,703
It behaves like a queue with suspending send and receive operations, and each element is normally consumed by one receiver.

36
00:02:23,569 --> 00:02:24,969
<i>How is Channel different from Flow?</i>

37
00:02:26,586 --> 00:02:30,076
A regular Flow is usually a cold declarative stream started by each collector.

38
00:02:30,776 --> 00:02:34,766
Channel is a hot point-to-point communication primitive: it exists independently of receivers,

39
00:02:34,956 --> 00:02:36,976
and each sent element is consumed by only one of them.

40
00:02:38,234 --> 00:02:39,834
<i>How is Channel different from SharedFlow?</i>

41
00:02:41,339 --> 00:02:44,179
Channel normally delivers each element to one competing receiver.

42
00:02:44,879 --> 00:02:49,519
SharedFlow broadcasts an emission to all active collectors and can replay values according to its configuration.

43
00:02:51,028 --> 00:02:53,058
<i>When should Channel be used instead of SharedFlow?</i>

44
00:02:54,579 --> 00:02:58,529
Use Channel when queue or single-consumer semantics matter, such as producer-consumer pipelines,

45
00:02:58,719 --> 00:03:00,779
work distribution or actor-like communication.

46
00:03:01,489 --> 00:03:04,139
Use SharedFlow when every active collector should observe the same emission;

47
00:03:04,309 --> 00:03:07,449
neither primitive guarantees durable UI-event delivery across process death.

48
00:03:08,982 --> 00:03:11,232
<i>What is the difference between Flow and a suspend function?</i>

49
00:03:12,763 --> 00:03:15,023
A suspend function usually returns one result.

50
00:03:15,723 --> 00:03:18,963
Flow can emit many values over time and is suitable for observing changes.

51
00:03:20,494 --> 00:03:22,464
<i>What is the difference between cold and hot Flow?</i>

52
00:03:24,011 --> 00:03:26,201
A cold Flow starts separately for every collector.

53
00:03:26,911 --> 00:03:30,581
A hot Flow exists independently of collectors; StateFlow and SharedFlow are hot flows.

54
00:03:32,102 --> 00:03:34,332
<i>What is the difference between collect and collectLatest?</i>

55
00:03:35,830 --> 00:03:37,880
collect processes every value to completion.

56
00:03:38,590 --> 00:03:41,630
collectLatest cancels the previous processing when a new value arrives.

57
00:03:43,513 --> 00:03:45,143
<i>When should flatMapLatest be used?</i>

58
00:03:46,410 --> 00:03:50,000
Use it when a new input value should switch to a new inner Flow and cancel the previous one.

59
00:03:50,700 --> 00:03:54,450
A common example is search by changing query or observing data for a selected ID.

60
00:03:55,961 --> 00:03:57,841
<i>What is the difference between combine and zip?</i>

61
00:03:59,363 --> 00:04:02,723
combine emits when any source changes and uses the latest values from the others.

62
00:04:03,433 --> 00:04:06,653
zip combines values pair by pair and waits for a new item from every source.

63
00:04:08,169 --> 00:04:09,059
<i>What is StateFlow?</i>

64
00:04:10,587 --> 00:04:12,467
StateFlow is a hot flow for holding state.

65
00:04:13,167 --> 00:04:17,067
It always has a current value, and a new collector immediately receives the latest value.

66
00:04:18,606 --> 00:04:19,496
<i>What is SharedFlow?</i>

67
00:04:21,014 --> 00:04:23,844
SharedFlow is a configurable hot flow for broadcasting values.

68
00:04:24,554 --> 00:04:27,934
It is often used for events and effects, with configurable replay and buffering.

69
00:04:29,446 --> 00:04:30,766
<i>StateFlow or SharedFlow?</i>

70
00:04:32,291 --> 00:04:36,441
StateFlow is suitable for state that must always have a current value and be available to new collectors.

71
00:04:37,151 --> 00:04:40,241
SharedFlow is more commonly used for events, signals, and one-off effects.

72
00:04:41,774 --> 00:04:43,304
<i>What is lifecycle-aware collection?</i>

73
00:04:44,811 --> 00:04:48,201
It means collecting a Flow only while the screen is in an appropriate lifecycle state.

74
00:04:48,901 --> 00:04:54,148
In Compose, state is usually collected with collectAsStateWithLifecycle(), while the View system commonly uses repeatOnLifecycle().
